d":"U_kgDOB1Z5Gg","gravatar_id":"","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"content_type":"text/plain; charset=utf-8","state":"uploaded","size":7057,"digest":"sha256:d305aa4eeb4266209cabdb01933e8ff84b0360a731ab7f4818a3bd5f2d3c6bed","download_count":13,"created_at":"2026-11-21T11:18:57Z","updated_at":"2026-11-21T11:18:57Z"},{"id":775332664,"node_id":"RA_kwDOIvux3s4uNqM4","name":"SHASUMS512.txt","label":"","uploader":{"login":"mise-en-dev","id":123107610,"node_id":"U_kgDOB1Z5Gg","gravatar_id":"","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"content_type":"text/plain; charset=utf-8","state":"uploaded","size":6175,"digest":"sha256:a8acd76ed0abc487ec66ef657971eda09affda59ad502b9584e566a4b619900b","download_count":20,"created_at":"2026-11-21T11:18:57Z","updated_at":"2026-11-21T11:18:57Z"},{"id":775332676,"node_id":"RA_kwDOIvux3s4uNqNE","name":"SHASUMS512.txt.minisig","label":"","uploader":{"login":"mise-en-dev","id":123107610,"node_id":"U_kgDOB1Z5Gg","gravatar_id":"","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"content_type":"application/octet-stream","state":"uploaded","size":309,"digest":"sha256:0179624bbe702b593a6afd0af87e40ef9929aed62c1231122fa7acf5f3c60cfd","download_count":18,"created_at":"2026-11-21T11:18:57Z","updated_at":"2026-11-21T11:18:57Z"},{"id":775333632,"node_id":"RA_kwDOIvux3s4uNqcA","name":"v2026.11.12.tar.gz.sig","label":"","uploader":{"login":"mise-en-dev","id":123107610,"node_id":"U_kgDOB1Z5Gg","gravatar_id":"","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"content_type":"application/pgp-signature","state":"uploaded","size":566,"digest":"sha256:eb75d49f35536d3563756af226db9ab1f64df0cdfa084eed189b78079a384966","download_count":19,"created_at":"2026-11-21T11:19:23Z","updated_at":"2026-11-21T11:19:23Z"}],"body":"New `mise audit` and `mise upgrade --vulnerable` commands check your tools against OSV.dev advisories and upgrade the affected ones. This release also adds `mise prune --system`, a per-tool `bump = false` option, and more flexible dotfile groups. It fixes lockfile handling for platforms a tool has no build for, PATH setup for locked task tools, and Temurin hotfix versions from `.sdkmanrc`.\n\n## Added\n\n### Vulnerability auditing\n\n- **`mise audit`** checks each tool version your config uses against the [OSV.dev](https://osv.dev) database and lists the advisories that affect it. It exits 1 when it finds any, so you can use it to gate CI. `mise audit node python` checks only the tools you name, and `--json` prints `{\"vulnerabilities\": [...], \"unchecked\": [...]}`. [#15942](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15942) (@jdx, @johnstegeman)\n\n  ```console\n  $ mise audit\n  Tool        Version  ID                   Severity  Fixed    Summary\n  npm:lodash  4.17.15  GHSA-35jh-r3h4-6jhm  high      4.17.21  Command Injection in lodash\n  ...\n  mise not checked, no known advisory source: zig@0.13.0\n  ```\n\n  - These tools are covered:\n    - registry packages (`npm:`, `pipx:`, `cargo:`, `gem:`, `go:`, `dotnet:`)\n    - tools released from GitHub or GitLab (`github:`, `aqua:`, `packslip:`, `gitlab:`), matched by repository and release tag\n    - the core tools go (as the Go standard library), node, python, deno, bun and erlang\n  - Anything else, including tools from private registries or self-hosted `api_url`s, is listed as **not checked** and is never reported as clean.\n  - The audit fails if a configured version doesn't resolve. It needs network access to `api.osv.dev`, so it fails in offline mode. Only tool names and versions are sent.\n- **`mise upgrade --vulnerable`** limits `mise upgrade` to installed tools with a known vulnerability. Upgrades stay within the configured range unless you add `--bump`. `--dry-run`, `--exclude` and `--inactive` work as usual. mise warns when an affected tool has no fix in range, when the target version is still affected, and when a tool could not be checked. [#15942](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15942) (@jdx, @johnstegeman)\n\n### Upgrades and pruning\n\n- **Per-tool `bump` option.** `bump = false` keeps a tool out of `mise upgrade --bump` and `mise outdated --bump`. Those commands still upgrade it within its version request but don't rewrite its entry in `mise.toml`. This helps when tools like topgrade run `mise upgrade` for you and can't pass `-x`. If you name the tool (`mise upgrade node --bump`), it is still bumped. This option isn't available in `.tool-versions`. [#15947](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15947) (@jdx, @gitfool)\n\n  ```toml\n  [tools]\n  node = { version = \"lts\", bump = false }\n  ```\n\n- **`mise prune --system`** removes unused versions from the system installs directory, using `sudo` when that directory is owned by root. A version is kept if a config file your user has loaded (including `/etc/mise/config.toml`) still selects it, or if a running process you can inspect uses it. mise can't see other users' configs, so it warns about that. Plain `mise prune` still leaves system installs alone. [#15949](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15949) (@jdx, @ioweb-gr)\n\n  ```sh\n  mise prune --system --dry-run\n  mise prune --system\n  ```\n\n  `mise upgrade` now also cleans up system installs it replaces, after the `upgrade.prune_after` grace period. Before, old versions piled up indefinitely. This background cleanup never asks for a sudo password. If removing a version would need `sudo`, mise keeps it and tells you to run `mise prune --system` or upgrade with `--prune`. `mise uninstall` of a system version in a root-owned directory now uses `sudo` instead of failing. `mise reshim --system` no longer drops the shim for a system version that you also have in your own installs directory.\n\n### Dotfiles\n\n- `root` in a `[dotfile_groups.<name>]` table now defaults to the group name. A table that only sets options such as `exclude` now deploys instead of failing with `root: is required`. Invalid groups no longer print a second, misleading \"no table declares it\" warning. [#15953](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15953) (@jdx, @lhs-12)\n- A dotfile group entry can now deploy outside the group's `target`, so a package whose files go to more than one place can be a single group. These entries need an explicit `source`, unless their mode takes none (such as `absent`). They are applied, unapplied and pruned with the rest of the group. The docs now also show how to deploy only a group's entries with `exclude = [\"/*\"]`. [#15954](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15954) (@jdx, @lhs-12)\n\n  ```toml\n  [dotfile_groups.vscode]\n  root = \"vscode/.config\"\n  target = \"~/AppData/Roaming\"\n\n  [dotfile_groups.vscode.entries]\n  \"~/.crossnote\" = { source = \"crossnote\", mode = \"symlink\" }\n  ```\n\n### Daemons\n\n- A daemon project reference now exports `<NAME>_PORT` and `<NAME>_URL` under its local name. For example, `[daemons.search]` with `project = \"../search\"` provides `SEARCH_PORT` and `SEARCH_URL`. With pitchfork 2.36.0 or later, `{{ daemons.search.url }}` also resolves in templates. Older pitchfork versions still get plain dependency IDs. Local daemons win when names collide. [#15956](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15956) (@jdx, @jholzinger-hadrian)\n\n## Fixed\n\n- **Lockfiles and platforms with no build.**\n  - `mise lock` now records platforms a tool has no build for in a new `unsupported_platforms` list in `mise.lock`. `mise lock --check` then passes for those platforms unless you name one with `--platform`. Before, `--check` failed on a lockfile that `mise lock` had just written. `mise install --locked` on a listed platform now reports that there is no build, instead of \"No lockfile URL found\". Auto-lock no longer resolves listed platforms again on every install. Node (with `node.compile` off) and PyPy now report missing builds this way too. The lockfile format version is unchanged. Older mise versions ignore the field but drop it if they rewrite the file. [#15952](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15952), [#15941](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15941) (@jdx, @NVolcz)\n  - In the default generate mode, `mise lock` now refuses a version upgrade that would drop a platform the old version supported. Before, it silently pruned the old version and wrote a lockfile without that platform. [#15939](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15939) (@jdx, @NVolcz)\n- **Locked task tools.** A tool pinned in a task's `tools` and locked with `mise lock` is now on the task's `PATH` on a fresh machine. Before, it was installed but missing from `PATH`, so the task failed or ran another copy of the command. [#15958](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15958) (@jdx, @hajikelu)\n- **Secrets in tasks.** Tasks can now list keys from your own fnox config, and tasks in your global or system mise config can list secrets. Both used to be refused, so you had to pass `mise run --secrets KEY` on every run. `--secrets-all` still leaves your own keys out unless the task names them. Remote tasks still can't list secrets. As a result, `mise secrets ls --json` and `mise tasks validate` no longer report the problem kinds `personal` or `not_project_config`. [#15937](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15937) (@jdx)\n- **Java.** `.sdkmanrc` entries for Temurin hotfix releases, such as `java=25.0.5.1+2-tem`, install again. They now resolve to the canonical catalog version (`temurin-25.0.5.1+2`). [#15928](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15928) (@jdx)\n- **`mise deps`.** The `aube` provider now watches `aube-workspace.yaml` (and `pnpm-workspace.yaml` when aube falls back to it) as well as each workspace member's `package.json`. Edits to catalogs, overrides or member dependencies are no longer reported as \"fresh\". [#15940](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15940) (@jdx)\n\n## Documentation\n\n- The `mise watch` help now explains that tasks separated by `:::` share one watcher, so a change to any task's sources reruns all of them. To restart tasks independently, use a separate `mise watch` per task. [#15938](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15938) (@jdx)\n\n## Breaking Changes\n\n- **Temurin hotfix versions.** The Java catalog now lists Temurin hotfixes under their canonical version. If you pinned an old encoded hotfix string such as `temurin-25.0.4+101`, change it to `temurin-25.0.4.1+1`. [#15928](https://github.com/jdx/mise/pull/15928)\n\n**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/jdx/mise/compare/v2026.11.11...v2026.11.12\n\n## 💚 Sponsor mise\n\nmise is built and maintained by [@jdx](https://github.com/jdx), an open source developer at [**entire.io**](https://entire.io/), the title sponsor of his open source work.\n\nIf mise saves you or your team time, please consider becoming an [individual or company sponsor](https://jdx.dev/sponsors.html). Your support funds ongoing development and helps keep mise fast, free, and independent.\n","reactions":{"total_count":12,"+1":5,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":4,"rocket":3,"eyes":0},"mentions_count":8}
